<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Han Shizhong</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1089–1151</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Han Shizhong was a general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> and one of the leading commanders who defended the Southern Song against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> Jin. In 1130 his fleet blockaded a Jin army returning north at Huangtiandang on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> for over forty days. He opposed peace with the Jin, and after the chief minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> Gui made peace and had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yue-fei">Yue Fei</a> executed in 1142 he retired from public life.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/han-shizhong.jpg" alt="Han Shizhong" width="320" height="711" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 劉松年繪 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E9%9F%93%E4%B8%96%E5%BF%A0.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Han Shizhong was born to a poor farming family in a village of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a> in 1089. In 1105, when Western Xia attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, he enlisted in the army of Yan'an Prefecture and became known for his archery and courage. During a stalemate at Yinzhou he broke into the city himself and killed one of its defending generals, which spurred the Song army to take it. When his superiors reported his deeds and asked for his promotion, Tong Guan, then in charge of border affairs, doubted the report and agreed only to make him a lieutenant.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1125 he helped suppress bandits and rebels in Damingfu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>. In 1127 he was given a thousand men to escort <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-gaozong-of-song">Zhao Gou</a>, Prince of Kang, to safety in the south, where the prince took the throne as Emperor Gaozong and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Southern Song</a>. Han urged the emperor to recover the lost northern lands, but his advice was not taken. In 1129 he argued against withdrawing to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunan">Hunan</a>, holding that the Huaihe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a> regions were rich and essential, and in 1131 he put down the peasant rising of Fan Ruwei, taking Jianzhou after a siege of six days.</p><p>In 1134, stationed at Zhenjiang as envoy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a>, Zhenjiang and Huaidong, he set ambushes in the swamps near Dayi Town and lured a Jin cavalry force into them, capturing over 200 Jin officers, among them the general Ta Boye. The Battle of Dayi Town was counted among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Southern Song</a>'s Thirteen Military Achievements. After further victories in 1140, at Jikou near Huaiyang, he was made Grand Tutor and Duke of Ying.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yue-fei">Yue Fei</a> was imprisoned, Han asked the chancellor on what charges; the reply, that no evidence was needed, became a Chinese proverb for a trumped-up charge. The execution showed Han how far corruption had spread at court, and he retired from military service. He died on 16 September 1151. The History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> praised him as a warrior equal to 10,000 men, and Yang Wanli named him with Yue Fei among the generals the Jin feared. He was credited with military inventions, among them modified bows and armour, a jumping course for training cavalry and an archery range. His wife, Liang Hongyu, was also known for her military ability.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Han Shizhong” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jin forces pursued the new Southern Song court south of the Yangtze in 1129–1130 but were checked by Song generals such as <b>Han Shizhong</b> and Yue Fei, and the Jin governed part of the conquered land through the puppet state of Qi from 1130 to 1137.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty (1115-1234) · Military</a></li></ul></section>
